Basic build/setup instructions for testing
------------------------------------------

1) Run 'mvn install'. The .war file webmaths.war will be generated inside the
   'target' folder.

2) Eclipse IDE will automatically compile the project assuming it is recognised
   as a Maven project.

3) To deploy to Tomcat 9, please install or upload the war file manually.

4) Once you have installed the application, you can test it is running by going
   to its root URL. This will take you to a status page.

NOTE: This app currently requires Tomcat 9.x.

Manually providing the Node.js app
----------------------------------

Using the basic setup, the app will install its own Node.js application in the
Tomcat user's home directory when it starts up, and update the app if necessary.
This may not be appropriate, especially on production servers, where the Tomcat
user might not have the necessary permissions to run 'npm install' and download
software etc.

You can build the Node.js app manually:

* Copy the files from src/main/resources/ou-mathjax into any suitable directory.
* In that directory, run 'npm install'
* Build the .war file using the command:

  mvn install -Dou-mathjax-location=/opt/ou-mathjax

  (Where /opt/ou-mathjax is replaced by whatever directory you built the app in.)

The included .spec file can be used if you want to build a .rpm to install
ou-mathjax, which is how we deploy it (you will need to make minor modifications
to the file which our build pipeline does).

How to write clients
--------------------

To get the JAX-WS information page for any service once you have installed it
on your server, go to http://your.server.example.com/webmaths/ and follow the
'Web services' link - you'll get a list of all eight services together with the
WSDL files that should be all you need in order to write software that uses the
services. The WSDL files contain documentation for all parameters and return
values.

The WSDL files are WS-I compliant so should be usable from all programming
languages.
